export anchor Escapes:
    description:
        Emul escapes special characters the same way Piton does. It is the
        only escape mechanism; there are no backslash sequences such as `\ \n \`
        or `\ \" \`.

    wrapper:
        To escape special characters, wrap them in backslashes with a space on
        each side. The spaces are part of the wrapper, so they are removed
        along with the backslashes. Even a single character is wrapped this
        way.

    specialCharacters:
        In Emul, escaping is needed for anything that would otherwise be read
        as syntax:

        - quotes
            A double quote inside quoted text, which would otherwise end the
            text. It is the only character quoted text needs escaped; braces
            and commas inside quotes are already just text.
        - braces
            A curly brace in an unquoted attribute value, which would otherwise
            open or close a body.
        - commas
            A comma in an unquoted attribute value, which would otherwise end
            the entry. Quoting the value is usually simpler.
        - comments
            A `//` at the start of a line that should be text rather than a
            comment.

    stacking:
        Backslashes can be stacked to escape backslashes themselves. The
        closing wrapper has to match the opening one, so a wrapper of two
        backslashes can contain a single backslash.

    multiLine:
        Putting the backslashes on a line by themselves opens a block, and the
        same line closes it. Everything in between is kept as it is, line
        breaks and indentation included. Emul uses three backslashes for these
        blocks, as Piton does.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        composition Pricing {
            Quote { "She said \ " \ worth it \ " \ and bought two" }
            Price { "From $5, billed monthly", title: Plans\ , \ pricing }
        }
        \\\
        ```

        The first instance shows the text `She said "worth it" and bought
        two`. In the second, the comma in the quoted text needs no escape, but
        the comma in the unquoted `title` attribute does, giving it the value
        `Plans, pricing`.
